content.content:
--- ROOT CAUSE ---
Category (ordinal) axis ticks are memoized per axis instance, but `Axis#getTicksCoords` never re-checks them against the axis's current scale extent, so when the visible window shrinks (dataZoom narrowing the scale extent on a live axis) the stale ticks are mapped through `dataToCoord` and land outside the axis coord extent / grid rect.

Detail:
1. `src/coord/axisTickLabelBuilder.ts:184-224` `makeCategoryTicks` computes ticks from `ordinalScale.getExtent()` and stores them in a per-axis cache keyed only by the tick `interval` option (`getListCache`/`listCacheGet`, lines 244-262). The cache is explicitly assumed to be safe because "axis is created each time during a ec process, so we do not need to clear cache" (comment at lines 241-243). That assumption breaks as soon as the scale extent changes on an existing axis — which is exactly the dataZoom window semantics, and what the requirement's regression test does (`axis.scale.setExtent(2, 4)`).
2. `src/coord/Axis.ts:179-188` maps every returned tick to a coord with no extent test whatsoever. With scale extent [2,4] and cached ticks [0,1,2,3,4] the mapping yields coords -150,-50,50,150,250 for a [0,300] axis.
3. The only clipping in the whole path is inside `fixOnBandTicksCoords` (`src/coord/Axis.ts:291-346`), and it is doubly insufficient:
   - it early-returns for `!axis.onBand || alignWithLabel` (line 296), so `boundaryGap:false` axes and `axisTick.alignWithLabel:true` axes get no clipping at all;
   - its clamp block (`src/coord/Axis.ts:326-337`) removes/rewrites exactly ONE tick per end (`ticksCoords.shift()` / `ticksCoords.pop()`), so when 2+ ticks are out of range the remainder still overflows; and with `clamp:true` it only rewrites `ticksCoords[0].coord = axisExtent[0]`, leaving the second out-of-range tick in place (hence the observed [0,-100,0,...]).
`getMinorTicksCoords` (`src/coord/Axis.ts:199-221`) has no clipping either, but it is not affected: `scale.getMinorTicks()` is always derived from the live extent (verified for Interval and Log, before/after `setExtent`), so no minor-tick overflow was observed.
--- CODE LOCATIONS ---
/workspace/src/coord/Axis.ts:169-197 (getTicksCoords; the unfiltered tick->coord map at 179-188 is where the fix belongs)
/workspace/src/coord/Axis.ts:291-346 (fixOnBandTicksCoords: early return at 296 for !onBand/alignWithLabel; single-shift/single-pop clamp handling at 326-337)
/workspace/src/coord/Axis.ts:199-221 (getMinorTicksCoords: no clipping, but empirically not a source of overflow)
/workspace/src/coord/axisTickLabelBuilder.ts:184-224 (makeCategoryTicks: builds ticks from the ordinal extent and caches them)
/workspace/src/coord/axisTickLabelBuilder.ts:241-262 (getListCache/listCacheGet/listCacheSet plus the 'no need to clear cache' comment — the stale-tick source)
/workspace/src/component/axis/AxisBuilder.ts:672 (buildAxisTick consumer), /workspace/src/component/axis/CartesianAxisView.ts:138 and /workspace/src/component/axis/axisSplitHelper.ts:54 (splitLine/splitArea consumers using clamp:true)
/workspace/test/ut/spec/coord/axisTicksInsideExtent.test.ts:60-90 (the two currently failing assertions; received -100 at both)
--- PROPOSED FIX ---
Filter out ticks that fall outside the axis's current scale extent in `Axis#getTicksCoords`, before mapping them to coords — i.e. fix it at the source of the coords, not inside `fixOnBandTicksCoords`. Restrict the filter to the ordinal scale, because that is the only tick path that is cached (and therefore the only one that can go stale), and its tick values/extents are integers so the comparison is exact.

Replace the `map(...)` block at src/coord/Axis.ts:179-188 with:

```ts
        const scale = this.scale;
        const isOrdinal = scale.type === 'ordinal';
        // The ticks of a category axis are cached (see `axisTickLabelBuilder`),
        // so they may have been made for a wider scale extent than the current
        // one (for example, before dataZoom narrowed the window). Those ticks
        // are out of the visible window and would be rendered outside the grid,
        // so discard them here. Ticks of the other scale types are always made
        // from the current extent.
        const scaleExtent = scale.getExtent();
        const ticksCoords: TickCoord[] = [];
        for (let i = 0; i < ticks.length; i++) {
            const tickVal = ticks[i];
            if (isOrdinal && (tickVal < scaleExtent[0] || tickVal > scaleExtent[1])) {
                continue;
            }
            ticksCoords.push({
                coord: this.dataToCoord(
                    isOrdinal ? (scale as OrdinalScale).getRawOrdinalNumber(tickVal) : tickVal
                ),
                tickValue: tickVal
            });
        }
```
(the rest of `getTicksCoords` — `alignWithLabel` lookup and the `fixOnBandTicksCoords(this, ticksCoords, alignWithLabel, opt.clamp)` call — stays untouched, so clamp:true semantics for splitLine/splitArea are preserved).

Why here and not elsewhere (measured, variant-by-variant):
- Verified numerically against a replica of the current algorithm. This fix ('B') yields exactly the values the regression test wants: non-clamp [0,100,200,300] and clamp [0,100,200,300] with coords[0]===extent[0]===0 and last===extent[1]===300; it also cleans up the redundant/duplicated ticks (window [1,3] -> [0,100,200,300] instead of [0,100,200,300,300]) and window [3,3] -> [0,300] (correct single band) instead of six ticks at 150.
- The alternative of looping the clamp removal inside `fixOnBandTicksCoords` (variant 'A': `while (ticksCoords.length > 1 && !littleThan(axisExtent[0], ticksCoords[1].coord)) ticksCoords.shift();` + symmetric pop) also satisfies the two failing assertions, but measurably does NOT fix `boundaryGap:false` ([-300,-150,0,150,300] unchanged) nor `alignWithLabel:true` ([-150,-50,50,150,250] unchanged), because that function early-returns for those. So it is a strictly weaker fix.
- Fixing it in `createAxisTicks`/`makeCategoryTicks` would require either mutating/copying the memoized array on every call (perf-sensitive path for large category data) or extending the cache key with the extent; filtering at the consumer keeps the cache untouched.
- No change is needed in `getMinorTicksCoords`: `scale.getMinorTicks()` is recomputed from the live extent and produced no out-of-extent coords in any probe.

Non-regression evidence for 'does not change non-zoomed charts': across the whole 4300+ config chart sweep (all axis types, all filterModes, inverse, min/max/scale/minInterval/interval/splitNumber, customValues, singleAxis, polar, y/both-axis zoom, before and after 6 different dataZoom actions), zero ordinal ticks ever fell outside `scale.getExtent()` — so the filter is a provable no-op for freshly built axes. Nine fresh-axis tick configurations (interval 0/1/2, count 1/2/5, alignWithLabel, onBand=false, inverse, clamp on/off) produce byte-identical coord arrays with and without the filter.
--- RISKS ---
Do NOT make the filter unconditional (all scale types). The sweep caught a real counterexample: log axis with min/max fixed and filterMode 'none' returns tick value 333333.9999999995 while scale.getExtent()[0] is 333333.99999999953 (pow/round round-trip noise). An unconditional `tickVal >= extent[0]` filter would delete that axis's min tick/splitLine. `round()` (10 decimals) happens to rescue this one case but not large-magnitude values, hence the `scale.type === 'ordinal'` guard.
If the narrowed window happens to contain none of the cached ticks (e.g. axisTick.interval:1 -> cached ticks [0,2,4], window [3,3]), the filtered list is empty, fixOnBandTicksCoords early-returns on `!ticksLen`, and getTicksCoords returns [] even with clamp:true — splitLine/splitArea then draw nothing where today they draw (wrong) lines. If the 'clamp always yields both extent bounds' guarantee must hold, add a guarded `if (clamp && !ticksLen) { return the two axisExtent bounds }` in fixOnBandTicksCoords; note ticks are also legitimately empty when the tick model has show:false, so such a branch must not fire for that case.
Ticks inserted by the clamp branches carry no `tickValue`; consumers already tolerate that (AxisBuilder.ts:645 anid, CartesianAxisView.ts:169-171, axisSplitHelper.ts:71/106-110 all null-check), and dropping out-of-window ticks changes anid identity for those elements, which can only alter axis transition animations, not layout.
The identical staleness exists for labels: getViewLabels() after a scale-extent narrowing still returns the old tickValues (measured coords -150 and -50 for a [0,300] axis), so axis labels would still be drawn outside the grid. The requirement only mentions ticks and the regression test only checks ticks; if label overflow is also in scope, the same filter is needed in createAxisLabels/makeCategoryLabels (or the label cache must be extent-aware).
Grid.ts rebuilds axes on every dataZoom action, so this bug is not visible in the built-in chart pipeline today — the fix is defensive/API-level. Any test that asserts a behavior change at the whole-chart level (dispatchAction + getTicksCoords) will pass both before and after the fix; the meaningful assertions are the Axis-level ones.
